        <p class="lead text-xl text-gray-700 font-medium">
          First episode of PerfEco Institutions: four articles on the projects of municipalities, provinces, public bodies and companies — how they are funded, structured and delivered. We start with what comes before everything else: the written project.
        </p>

        <h2>The sentence we hear everywhere</h2>

        <p>
          "We have the project. We just need the funding." We hear it in town halls and in companies alike, and it is sincere. Almost every time, after ten minutes of conversation, another reality appears: the project exists in people's heads, in meetings, sometimes in a letter. It does not yet exist on a page that an outsider could read and understand on their own.
        </p>

        <p>
          This is not a criticism. A useful project almost always starts from a real need, carried by people who know it well. What it lacks is the form that allows a funder to say yes.
        </p>

        <h2>Three approaches that start from good intentions</h2>

        <p>
          <strong>The funding window spotted first.</strong> A grant is open and the deadline is known, so the project is written to fit the boxes. The application ends up looking like the call, and less and less like what the organisation actually wanted to build.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>The application put together in three weeks.</strong> The submission date is close. Documents are gathered in a rush, appendices completed the night before. The file goes out, but the core questions remain open.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>The project everyone supports.</strong> Everyone finds it useful, elected officials and staff alike. Nobody has signed it, and nobody has said who will carry it once it starts.
        </p>

        <p>
          All three have one thing in common: the project is written at the same time as the money is requested. The funder receives a request before receiving a project.
        </p>

        <h2>What a funder checks before the amount</h2>

        <p>
          Before looking at the sum requested, a funder checks three things, in this order.
        </p>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>Who signs.</strong> The name of the person who commits the organisation, and of the person who will run the facility or service once it is built.</li>
          <li><strong>What is already ready.</strong> Studies, land, permits. What is missing shows up within two minutes of reading.</li>
          <li><strong>Your share.</strong> The funder wants to see it written into a budget that has already been voted, not just announced in a meeting.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>The four answers that make a project fundable</h2>

        <p>
          A project becomes fundable when it gives a clear answer to four questions.
        </p>

        <ol>
          <li><strong>Who it serves.</strong> How many people, and for what. It is often a figure the organisation already has.</li>
          <li><strong>Who carries it.</strong> A name — a person. A department does not carry a project: a person does.</li>
          <li><strong>What it costs.</strong> The construction, then every year that follows the launch.</li>
          <li><strong>Who will report.</strong> The person who will produce the supporting documents required at the end, and on what working time.</li>
        </ol>

        <div class="bg-gray-50 border-l-4 border-teal rounded-r-xl p-6 my-8">
          <p class="font-semibold text-gray-800 mb-1">Diagnostic question</p>
          <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm">On the project you talk about most, who signs the commitment? How much will it cost to run three years after it opens? And who will produce the documents the funder asks for?</p>
        </div>

        <h2>The cost of the third year</h2>

        <p>
          The third answer is the one most often forgotten. The construction is carefully costed, while maintenance, staff, energy and insurance are set aside. Yet it is this cost of the following years that decides whether the project holds once built. The funder looks at it last, and that is often where the answer becomes no.
        </p>

        <p>
          In New Caledonia, investment budgets are tighter than two years ago. The teams that used to prepare applications are not always replaced when they leave. Useful projects exist: they are waiting for a written page, and for someone with the time to write it.
        </p>

        <h2>Ten lines to write this week</h2>

        <p>
          You already carry useful projects, and you were right to carry them. Our advice: take the one you talk about most often, and write it in ten lines. Who it serves, who signs it, what it costs — the construction and the following years —, who will report.
        </p>

        <p>
          If one of the four lines stays empty, you know where to start. If all four are filled in, you already have the first page of your application, and it will hold whatever funding window you choose next.
        </p>

        <h2>What PerfEco Institutions does</h2>

        <p>
          PerfEco Institutions supports municipalities, provinces, public bodies and companies, from the first page of the project to the last supporting document: writing the project, costing it in full, naming who carries it and who will report, then following its delivery.
        </p>

        <p>
          No consultancy can guarantee that funding will be obtained. What you keep in every case: a mature project, a costed financing plan and a reusable dashboard.
        </p>

